# Sleela — Language Syntax Specification

    Language:        Sleela
    Version:         9.09.1
    Surface:         Java-like
    Core:            Turing-complete, thread-friendly C/C++ execution core
    Companion:       SHEET.sheet (system-object catalog), SST.model (NS-SST-0001)
    Status:          Normative for the v9.09.1 surface

Sleela is a **Java-like** programming language. Its programs are classes of
fields and methods that compile to bytecode on the C/C++ core. Beyond ordinary
Java-like code, Sleela can **address every object type named in `SHEET.sheet`**
through its *conducted methods*, and it honors the **Constitution** invariants
(system depth 3024, complexity degree ≤ 4).

This document is normative for the syntax the v9.09.1 front end accepts. Every
production and built-in below is implemented in `impl/frontend` and executes on
`impl/core`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 1. Lexical grammar

### 1.1 Encoding & trivia
Source is UTF-8. Whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF) separates tokens and is
otherwise insignificant. Two comment forms:

    // line comment — to end of line
    /* block comment — may span lines */

### 1.2 Keywords (reserved)

    class   static   void
    int     double   boolean   String
    if      else     while     for      return
    true    false    null      print

`print` is a reserved statement keyword (§4.6). The seven conducted-method and
six threading names (§6, §7) are **contextual built-ins**, not reserved words:
they are recognized in call position only, so they may otherwise be used as
identifiers.

### 1.3 Identifiers

    ident      ::= (letter | "_") (letter | digit | "_")*

Case-sensitive. Class names are conventionally `PascalCase`; methods and
variables `camelCase` (convention, not enforced).

### 1.4 Literals

    int-lit    ::= digit+                         // 64-bit signed
    double-lit ::= digit+ "." digit+
    bool-lit   ::= "true" | "false"
    string-lit ::= '"' (char | escape)* '"'       // escapes: \n \t \r \\ \"
    null-lit   ::= "null"

### 1.5 Operators & punctuation

    arithmetic   +   -   *   /   %
    comparison   ==  !=  <   <=  >   >=
    logical      &&  ||  !
    assignment   =
    grouping     (   )   {   }
    separators   ;   ,

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 2. Program structure

    program    ::= class-decl+
    class-decl ::= "class" ident "{" member* "}"
    member     ::= field-decl | method-decl

A program is one or more classes. Execution begins at the method named `main`
(signature `void main()`); exactly one `main` is required across the program.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 3. Types & declarations

### 3.1 Types

    type       ::= "int" | "double" | "boolean" | "String" | "void"

`int` is 64-bit. `void` is a return type only. The core is dynamically typed
underneath; v9.09.1 type annotations are surface-level (parsed, not enforced),
so a declared type documents intent without constraining the runtime value.

### 3.2 Fields (shared state)

    field-decl ::= ["static"] type ident ["=" expr] ";"

A field is **class-level shared state** and compiles to a thread-safe core
global. Fields declared with initializers are initialized once, in order, at the
top of `main`. Because fields are shared, they are the correct place for state
that spawned threads (§7) read or write.

### 3.3 Methods

    method-decl ::= ["static"] type ident "(" params? ")" block
    params      ::= param ("," param)*
    param       ::= type ident

`static` is accepted and optional; all methods behave as static (bound to the
class, not an instance). Method **locals** are per-thread; **fields** are shared.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 4. Statements

    block      ::= "{" statement* "}"
    statement  ::= block
                 | var-decl
                 | assign
                 | if-stmt
                 | while-stmt
                 | for-stmt
                 | return-stmt
                 | print-stmt
                 | expr-stmt

### 4.1 Local variable declaration
    var-decl   ::= type ident ["=" expr] ";"

### 4.2 Assignment
    assign     ::= ident "=" expr ";"
Resolves to a local if one is in scope, otherwise to a field (a local shadows a
field of the same name).

### 4.3 If / else
    if-stmt    ::= "if" "(" expr ")" statement ["else" statement]

### 4.4 While
    while-stmt ::= "while" "(" expr ")" statement

### 4.5 For
    for-stmt   ::= "for" "(" [simple] ";" [expr] ";" [simple] ")" statement
    simple     ::= var-decl-noSemi | assign-noSemi | expr

### 4.6 Print (built-in output)
    print-stmt ::= "print" "(" expr ")" ";"
Renders the value and appends a newline; each printed line is atomic across
threads.

### 4.7 Return
    return-stmt ::= "return" [expr] ";"

### 4.8 Expression statement
    expr-stmt  ::= expr ";"
Typically a call (including a conducted-method or threading built-in used for
its effect).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 5. Expressions (precedence, lowest → highest)

    expr        ::= logic-or
    logic-or    ::= logic-and   ("||" logic-and)*
    logic-and   ::= equality    ("&&" equality)*
    equality    ::= comparison  (("==" | "!=") comparison)*
    comparison  ::= additive    (("<" | "<=" | ">" | ">=") additive)*
    additive    ::= multiplic.  (("+" | "-") multiplic.)*
    multiplic.  ::= unary       (("*" | "/" | "%") unary)*
    unary       ::= ("-" | "!") unary | primary
    primary     ::= int-lit | double-lit | string-lit | bool-lit | null-lit
                  | ident
                  | call
                  | "(" expr ")"
    call        ::= ident "(" args? ")"
    args        ::= expr ("," expr)*

`+` is arithmetic on numbers and **concatenation** when either operand is a
`String` (Java-style). Comparisons and logical operators yield `boolean`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 6. Conducted methods — addressing `.sheet` object types

Sleela's defining v9.09.1 feature: a program can **address every object type in
`SHEET.sheet`** — all 16 role categories (root, start, idiom, highlight, call,
method, attention, gain, conversation, piping, transitive, completitive,
inheritance, smart-move, congruence, limit) and every object within them — via
*conducted methods*. These are contextual built-ins resolved against the catalog
at compile time; an object is named by a **string literal**.

    conduct("Name")        -> boolean   // is Name a catalogued object?
    role("Name")           -> String    // its conduct role (one of the 16)
    insight("Name")        -> String    // its insight/gloss
    congruent("A", "B")    -> boolean    // do A and B route to a congruence?
    route("A", "B")        -> String     // "A -> B" if congruent, else ""
    sysdepth()             -> int        // Constitution: system depth (3024)
    degreemax()            -> int        // Constitution: complexity degree max (4)

**Addressing any object type.** Because the argument is any catalogued name,
these built-ins reach the whole `SHEET.sheet` object space uniformly — there is
no per-type syntax to learn. Examples across roles:

    conduct("System")          // root
    role("Pipeline")           // -> "piping"
    role("CircuitBreaker")     // -> "smart-move"
    insight("Reward")          // gain: "accrued value guiding adaptive behavior."
    insight("Invariant")       // congruence/constraint
    congruent("Retry", "CircuitBreaker")   // same role -> true
    route("Pipeline", "Stream")            // siblings   -> "Pipeline -> Stream"

**Congruence rule.** `congruent(A, B)` is true when A and B share a conduct role
or are listed as each other's siblings in `SHEET.sheet` — this is the routable
relation used for system insight and connection.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 7. Threading (bounded, thread-friendly)

The core provides a bounded, clean concurrency model (up to **128** threads).
Sleela exposes it as contextual built-ins:

    spawn(method)          // start a no-arg method on a new thread; yields a thread id
    join()                 // wait for all spawned threads to finish
    lock(n)                // acquire lock-table slot n   (n a compile-time int, 0..31)
    unlock(n)              // release lock-table slot n
    send(slot, value)      // put the 2-tuple (slot, value) on the mailbox line
    recv(slot)             // block until a tuple arrives on slot; yields its value

Guidance: guard shared **field** access with `lock`/`unlock`; hand values
between threads over the 2-tuple mailbox with `send`/`recv`; `join()` before
reading final results. `print` output stays atomic per line.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 8. The Constitution (invariants in force)

Every Sleela program runs under the ordained invariants (see README §The
Constitution):

  * **System depth** = `sysdepth()` = **3024** — maximum reachability depth.
  * **Complexity degree** = `degreemax()` = **4** — maximum connection order
    (1 direct · 2 mediated · 3 brokered · 4 federated).
  * **Connect rule**: connecting `n` congruent-linear systems is permitted iff
    `n <= 3024` and `degree(topology) <= 4`.

These values are constants a program can read directly via the conducted
methods, so behavior can be gated on the system's own limits.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 9. Complete example

    // v9.09.1 — Java-like surface + conducted methods + threading.
    class Atlas {

        int hits = 0;                        // shared field (thread-safe global)

        void worker() {
            lock(0);
            hits = hits + 1;                 // guarded shared update
            unlock(0);
            send(1, 1);                      // announce completion on the burble line
        }

        void main() {
            // Address .sheet object types by name (any of the 16 roles).
            print("depth=" + sysdepth() + " degree<=" + degreemax());
            print(role("Pipeline") + ": " + insight("Pipeline"));
            print(route("Retry", "CircuitBreaker"));   // same role -> a route

            // Bounded concurrency: spawn 8 workers, drain, join.
            int i = 0;
            while (i < 8) { spawn(worker); i = i + 1; }
            int got = 0;
            while (got < 8) { recv(1); got = got + 1; }
            join();
            print("hits = " + hits);
        }
    }

Expected output (order of the concurrent phase aside):

    depth=3024 degree<=4
    piping: an ordered composition of transforming stages.
    Retry -> CircuitBreaker
    hits = 8

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 10. Reserved-word & built-in summary

    keywords (14):  class static void int double boolean String
                    if else while for return true false null print
    conduct (7):    conduct role insight congruent route sysdepth degreemax
    threading (6):  spawn join lock unlock send recv

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sleela 9.09.1 — Java-like surface, addressing the full SHEET.sheet object space,
on the Turing-complete threaded C/C++ core.
